%S A400771 1,8,6,2,13,3,4,9,12,19,5,7,24,33,14,18,11,31,25,17,40,30,59,50,42,20,
%T A400771 28,34,15,35,26,10,27,52,41,49,23,32,39,16,38,70,60,51,43,21,44,75,62,
%U A400771 53,45,76,63,73,84,99,61,71,58,29,57,48,22,47,82,97,85,74,64,36,65,102,87,130,86,72,83,98,114,101
%N A400771 Squares visited by knight moves on a diagonally numbered Q1 board and always taking the available unvisited square with the shortest Euclidean distance to the starting square (1,1). In the case of a tie, first squares with the shortest distance to the Q1 bisecting line are preferred, second the square with smaller row index is preferred. Starting square is labeled 1.
%C A400771 Board is numbered as follows:
%C A400771    1  2  4  7 11 16 ...
%C A400771    3  5  8 12 17 ...
%C A400771    6  9 13 18 ...
%C A400771   10 14 19 ...
%C A400771   15 20 ...
%C A400771   21 ...
%C A400771   ...
%C A400771 The knight's tour starts at position (1,1), the first square of the tour. The knight moves in the open field spanned by the positive x- and y-axis, i.e., the first quadrant Q1.
%C A400771 In case the knight has a choice between two squares, which have equal Euclidean distance to the starting square (1,1), first the square which is closest to the bisection line of the first quadrant is preferred. If this first tiebreaking rule still leaves an optional square, the square with its row index being smaller than the row index of the alternative square is chosen to break the tie.
%C A400771 The knight becomes trapped after 117 moves at position (10, 1).
%H A400771 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400771/b400771.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..118</a>
%H A400771 M. F. Hasler, <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/Knight_tours">Knight tours</a>, OEIS wiki, Nov. 2019.
%H A400771 N. J. A. Sloane and Brady Haran, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RGQe8waGJ4w">The Trapped Knight</a>, Numberphile video (2019).
%H A400771 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400771/a400771_1.svg">Illustration of the path</a>.
%H A400771 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400771/a400771.csv.txt">Array after the knight was trapped as csv-file</a>.
%H A400771 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400771/a400771.py.txt">Python program</a>.
%e A400771 Board after 14 steps: (0's are unvisited squares).
%e A400771   1   4   7  12   0 ...
%e A400771   6  11   2   9   0 ...
%e A400771   3   8   5   0  13 ...
%e A400771   0   0  10   0   0 ...
%e A400771   0   0   0  14   0 ...
%e A400771   ...
%e A400771 Positions are (row, column).
%e A400771 a(1) = 1 because 1 is at position (1,1) which is numbered 1.
%e A400771 a(2) = 8 because 2 is at position (2,3) which is numbered 8. In this case, the subordinate tiebreaking rule chooses (2,3) over the other available candidate (3,2), because both positions have equal Euclidean distance to the starting square (1,1) as well as to the bisecting line, but (2,3) exhibits the smaller row index.
%e A400771 a(3) = 6 because 3 is at position (3,1) which is numbered 6.
%e A400771 a(14) = 33 because 14 is at position (5,4) which is numbered 33. In this case, the first tiebreaking rule chooses (5,4) over the other available candidate (1,6) as (5,4) is closer to the bisecting line.
